this morning we're closer to seeing at  least part of a highly sensitive  affidavit explaining in detail why the  fbi wanted to search former president  trump's mar-a-lago home the federal  judge who signed the search warrant has  ordered the justice department to  propose a redacted document for public  release at the same time the trump  organization's former chief financial  officer has pleaded guilty in a new york  tax fraud case potentially making him a  witness in a future trial senior  investigative correspondent katherine  herridge is outside the justice  department catherine good morning  good morning vlad thursday's court  action in new york and florida could  have significant ramifications for the  former president who faces multiple  civil and criminal investigations  meantime the justice department has just  six days to propose what information  should be blacked out or redacted from  that affidavit the trump spokesman said  the entire record should be public  on thursday the federal judge who  approved the fbi search of former  president trump's mar-a-lago home almost  two weeks ago found the justice  department had not met the high  threshold for showing that the entire  affidavit should remain sealed but he  did order the immediate release of these  other procedural records that provided  some insight into the possible crimes  that led to the mar-a-lago search they  include willful retention of national  defense information concealment or  removal of government records and  obstruction of a federal investigation  we are entitled to monitor the affairs  of our government at all levels the  judge agreed with media organizations  including cbs news that at least some of  the affidavit that supports the  mar-a-lago search could be unsealed it  is up to the public to determine what is  meaningful in this investigation  prosecutors argued releasing any portion  of the affidavit would threaten their  investigation and the safety of  potential witnesses and 